      "reranked": false,
      "answer": "The cited note `DOCS/notes/20260906-repository-understanding-trace-and-structural-artifacts.md` records a historical incident.\n\nAccording to the repository-understanding note, the problem with the my_test_repo answer was that the RAG system returned the structure of a small `my_test_repo` fixture (a test repository) instead of the actual repository the user was asking about. This was classified as a \"repository-subject contamination / identity grounding failure\" (section 3.2).\n\nThe evidence shows that when a user asked for the actual repository directory/service/package structure, the system described `my_test_repo`, which was documented inside historical/test-result material. The note states that \"Fixture/example/archive text must not silently redefine repository identity\" - meaning the system should have used the selected `repo_id` as the subject of the query unless the user explicitly asked about another embedded/example repository.\n\nThe document explicitly identifies this as a problem with generic semantic top-k retrieval being a poor primitive for repository-global structural questions, not with the information itself being unknowable.",
